title the bound read at both edges -- Pond's spool cloth takes a desk, and one of its two call sites holds a copy inside the array
prompt The HUSH seat prompt: roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, rota row N mod 5, QA on touch, read scope, ListAgents before a file moves, claim-as-override when my lane's queue is empty. The card named my lane's door outright -- spool_cloth.rye carries the same max_name=48 and wall with no pedestal, Hush's.
think LISTAGENTS FIRST answered none reachable, so the process table named the peers by cwd: Dream runs Codex in ~/grain-dream, an eleven-day-parked Claude sits in the elder ~/grain, and no other hand held this checkout. git stash list was empty, the index was clean, and git fetch xy put me level with the pier at fe84add20a -- so %321's opening read was quiet and the card I read was the tree I stood on.
think ROTA ROW 2, FIRE, and I am naming the skip rather than papering it. N = git rev-list --count HEAD = 3872, 3872 mod 5 = 2; the previous lap read row 0, so row 1 (Air) goes unread between us. The seated rule permits a hand advance only past a REPEAT, and this is a jump, which is exactly the fault the seed's own passage measured -- seven jumps in nineteen recorded pairs, eleven rows unread. I read Fire. Its threshold asks the lap to look hard at the bright uncomfortable point rather than route around it, and to cut once.
think THE DOOR WAS NAMED AND I READ THE ROOM BEFORE BUILDING. Tablecloth's four Glow desks landed 20260829 and 20260830, and the last of them left the invitation on its own placard: pond/apps/spool_cloth.rye took the same wall in the same shape on the same day, its bounds are Pond's to display. So the shape and the guard family were already argued; what I owed was the reading that makes this desk its own rather than a copy.
think AND THERE IS ONE. This module reads max_name at TWO call sites, and only one of them is a policy. store_large refuses a long name with NameTooLong. parse_manifest refuses a long field with BadManifest -- and that one guards an array's end, because a parsed entry's name lands in a fixed [max_name]u8 field, so with the check gone a hand-authored manifest carrying a sixty-byte name is copied past the end of the array. A refusal downgraded to a panic. I proved the two fire apart before writing them as two readings: delete either call and the scan still reads the survivor as wired, which the control now checks by name while its neighbour refuses.
think THE ARITHMETIC IS MEASURED RATHER THAN DERIVED. The desk claims the shortest lawful large line is 76 bytes, which is what makes 48 safe by arithmetic rather than by a rule. I built the module (1s), ran its selftest GREEN, and measured the skeleton directly -- printf of the render format with a one-byte name, a 64-character digest and two single digits reads 76 without the newline. A number a reader can check beats a number a writer remembers.
think A SUSPECTED RED DISSOLVED UNDER THE SECOND LOOK, IN PUBLIC. The card's Git nib read 474e0bed51 while HEAD read fe84add20a, and the elder hash is contained in no branch and is an ancestor of no ref -- reflog only, one gc from gone -- with nib_honesty green over it. That looked like %388's class exactly. Then I read the scan, which already computes git_nib_reach=local-only and names the state expected_pre_amend_sibling by construction, with the reason written in its own comment. The convention is documented and the guard reports it honestly. No row booked, and the check is written down here so the next lap does not spend the same twenty minutes.
think ONE ACCURACY CORRECTION CAUGHT BEFORE IT LANDED. My first draft opened Pond's first pedestal. brushstroke/tablecloth.rye, which every Tablecloth desk cites, is a symlink into pond/apps/ -- so Tablecloth is a Pond app wearing a Brushstroke name and Pond had four desks already. The honest claim is narrower: this is the second half of one room's naming layer, and the first desk naming a pond/apps/ path outright. The readers line moved the same way, from a guessed Pond to the two modules that actually consume the bound, Spool Cloth and Spool Keyed.
think THE PLACARD'S SIXTH LINE HAS NEVER BEEN READ BY VALUE. PLACARD.md seats six lines and gives the last one its reason -- a pedestal always displays a value AT a nib. I checked rather than assumed: every placard reading in the tree -- the four *_glow_tend scans and src_first_resident_witness -- counts the `nib` KEYWORD in the six-line order and stops, so across the room's 37 standing residents the stamp after it is compared to nothing. A module whose version moves while its desk keeps the elder nib is a desk saying, in its own words, that it shows a value from a release the module has left. So this desk's nib stamp is compared against `spool_cloth_version`, an honest bump moving both walks free, and the drift refuses while every bound reading stays quiet -- which the control checks by name.
obs THE CONTROL FOUND A SHADOWED CASE IN ITS OWN FIRST RUN. Deleting the example line to reach desk_example_missing reds one reading earlier at placard_order, because the placard gathers six keywords and a deleted line leaves five. So the verdict was unreachable by the obvious plant. The case now writes the example in words rather than as a literal, which keeps the keyword in its seated place and leaves the value unreadable -- the fault that reading actually owns.
obs THE SAME SHADOW STANDS NEXT DOOR, UNPLANTED. tools/fixtures/t/tablecloth_glow_tend_control.sh has 44 cases and none of them plants a missing example, so four verdict branches -- desk_example_missing, content_example_missing, error_example_missing, name_example_missing -- carry no control case at all. I know why, because I met it: the plain plant lands on placard_wrong. Cross-lane, so it is a card line rather than my lap, and it is named with the mechanism so whoever takes it does not rediscover the shadow.
obs THE ELDER DESK READS ONE OF TWO EDGES TOO. tablecloth.rye's parse_manifest carries the identical `if (name.len > max_name) return error.BadManifest;` over the identical fixed [max_name]u8 field, and its scan reads only the store edge through wall_wired. Same lane as the row above, same card line. My desk carries the reading; the elder's is its owner's to add.
obs QA ON TOUCH, AND THE FAMILY CHECKED. Desk B+ 87, scan B+ 86, control A 92, witness B+ 89, all at the Field setting and all above the B door, so no molt frame pushed. Register runs low across the family because the subject genuinely is what a wall refuses; the sibling Tablecloth desk reads 85 the same way, so mine is in family rather than out of it. That comparison is why I spent no round rewriting prose the genre explains.
obs A THIRD FIND, NAMED AND LEFT ALONE. session-logs/date/README-index-20260831.md declares "Rows for 20260831, newest first" and its top row is 20260831.000928, the OLDEST lap of the day, standing above 031509 -- prepended in writing order rather than by stamp, which is %391's own class one shelf down from where %391 repaired it. I left it. The row is another body's, Dream is mid-lap in ~/grain-dream, and moving a row inside a shared shelf is exactly the in-flight work a claim never touches. Mine went in below the delimiter where it belongs, at 173 of 192 bytes.
obs THE ROSTER, MEASURED RATHER THAN ESTIMATED. THE COLD PASS RAN WHOLE, THE FIRST IN FOUR LAPS, AND THE NUMBER IS NOT WHAT ANY WINDOW SAID. 108 guards, 107 green, 1 red -- rule_twin alone at gate %7 -- in 2,774 seconds of wall against 2,772 seconds of guard time, so the runner's own overhead across a whole pass is TWO seconds and the pass simply IS its guards. Median 3 s, mean 25.7, max 840. One lap projected three and a half hours and my own last lap projected fifty-five minutes off 28 guards; the truth is forty-six. caravan_suite alone is 840 s -- 30 percent of the pass in one row, and it is Dream's lane -- and the top four (caravan_suite, sow, reds_row_present, living_card_ascii) hold 1,514 s, 55 percent of the whole, in 3.7 percent of the guards, while 35 guards cost a second or less. A distribution that shaped is why every window anyone sampled disagreed with every other, and it is now on the run card rather than in a paragraph.
obs AND THE SEND COLLIDED, WHICH IS THE DERIVED SPINE FIRING FOR THE EIGHTH TIME. I fetched xy and read `reds_spine_derive_scan.sh --next` as 392 immediately before booking, then held the tree still for a 43-minute hot pass, and the pier published its own %392 (20260831.031048) and %393 (20260831.035452) inside that window. A published number never moves and mine was unshared, so my row renumbered to %394 against upstream's head; the stamp 20260831.045858 never moved, because the stamp is the identity and the number is a view. %389 already named this class and its own mitigation -- check the shelf names against xy/main -- and this instance shows the mitigation's limit from a third side: the window is the ROUND, not the check, and a round that must hold still for 46 minutes to prove itself is a 46-minute window by construction. That is the same sentence %374 has been waiting on.
obs BOTH HALVES CLOSED WHOLE, WHICH THE RECORD DOES NOT SHOW HAPPENING BEFORE. Cold: 108 guards, 107 green, 2,774 s, tree_moved=no. Hot: 108 guards, 107 green, 2,568 s, tree_moved=no, over the 12 staged paths. The one red is rule_twin at gate %7 in both, so `roster_receipt_write=withheld_guard_red` and `run_verdict=guard_red` -- %374's shape exactly, and the whole cost of it is now a measured number rather than an estimate: 46 minutes a pass, twice a lap, is what a receipt would buy back.
obs THE HOT PASS TOOK THREE STARTS AND THE TWO WASTED ONES SHARE A ROOT: a hot pass reads DERIVED surfaces that a hook regenerates at commit, so anything the hook would write must be written BEFORE the pass, not after. Start one died at nib_honesty over my `PENDING___` placeholder; start two died at reds_ledger_monotone because booking %392 moved the spine past the headline the pin recites. Fifty-two guards paid for that lesson. I had refreshed README.md and docs-geode/libraries and stopped there; the hook's rule two owns the ledger headline and I had not read far enough down its own comment.
obs I BOOKED MY OWN RED, %394 AFTER A COLLISION, AND IT IS THE SHARPEST THING THIS LAP FOUND. The hot pass reddened at nib_honesty over a `PENDING___` placeholder I had left in the card's Git nib field -- correct, and the guard working exactly as written. I stopped the line to fix the root and stopped it with `pkill -TERM -f standing_equipment_run.sh`, which matches every process on the HOST. Three bodies on this pier run that exact script name. It killed my own background waiter too, which is how it announced itself: exit 144. Reading the process table afterward, a surviving `--hot` runner's /proc cwd named ~/grain-dream. The evidence says I reached only my own runner -- Dream's run card was written 05:08, before the kill, and its next pass began 05:13:04, after -- yet that is evidence rather than proof, and a kill needing forensics afterward is the wrong kill. Read scope bounds which files a lap walks and nothing bounds which processes it signals. I re-killed by pid, confirming ppid=1 for an orphaned local runner against Dream's supervisor pid.
obs THE HOT PASS PAID FOR THE PLACEHOLDER. Twenty-six guards in, stopped, fixed, and restarted from zero -- so this lap ran one whole cold pass, twenty-six wasted hot guards, and one more hot pass. The placeholder was mine: I passed a literal PENDING___ into the card patch meaning to fill it at the amend, where the seated ritual puts the ELDER hash in the work commit and lets the single amend replace it. The card now carries fe84add20a.
